1
00:05:34,736 --> 00:05:40,736
Sex, drugs and punk rock. Add violence and time travel and you have Jubilee.

2
00:11:09,473 --> 00:11:15,473
Jubilee is a comedy, drama, fantasy film directed by Derek Jarman.

3
00:16:44,210 --> 00:16:50,210
Queen Elizabeth I visits late 1970s England to find a depressing
landscape where life has changed since her time.

4
00:22:18,947 --> 00:22:24,947
Queen Elizabeth I / Bod, played by Jenny Runacre.

5
00:27:53,684 --> 00:27:59,684
Crabs (as Little Nell), played by Nell Campbell.

6
00:33:28,421 --> 00:33:34,421
Mad, played by Toyah Willcox.

7
00:39:03,157 --> 00:39:09,157
Amyl Nitrite, played by Pamela Rooke.

8
00:44:37,894 --> 00:44:43,894
Angel, played by Ian Charleson.

9
00:50:12,631 --> 00:50:18,631
Sphinx, played by Karl Johnson.

10
00:55:47,368 --> 00:55:53,368
Jubilee is a 1978 British drama film directed by Derek Jarman.

11
01:01:22,105 --> 01:01:28,105
It stars Jenny Runacre, Ian Charleson, Nell Campbell,
Hermine Demoriane and a host of punk rockers.

12
01:06:56,842 --> 01:07:02,842
The title refers to the Silver Jubilee of Elizabeth II in 1977.

13
01:12:31,578 --> 01:12:37,578
Numerous punk icons appear in the film including Adam Ant, Toyah, Jordan (a
Malcolm McLaren protégé), Gene October and Jayne County.

14
01:18:06,315 --> 01:18:12,315
It features performances by Jayne County and Adam and the Ants.

15
01:23:41,052 --> 01:23:47,052
There are also cameo appearances by the Slits and Siouxsie and the Banshees.

16
01:29:15,789 --> 01:29:21,789
The film was scored by Brian Eno.

17
01:34:50,526 --> 01:34:56,526
The film explores themes of police brutality, street
gang, police state, group sex, dark comedy.

18
01:40:25,263 --> 01:40:31,263
The tone is lighthearted and dark and emotional.
